\pard\widctlpar\li360\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf4\ulnone\fs24 1. What are the processes of the ecosystem influenced by landscape structure? 2\par
\cf0\b0\lang2057 A landscape is a heterogeneous area composed of several ecosystems. The ecosystems making up a landscape generally form a mosaic of visually distinctive patches. These patches are called landscape\~elements.\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sa160\sl360\slmult1\qj\b Landscape structure influences processes such as the flow of energy, materials, and species between the ecosystems within a landscape\b0 .\~Landscape ecologists have proposed that landscape structure, especially the size, number,\~and isolation of habitat patches, can influence the movement\~of organisms between potentially suitable habitats. The\~group of subpopulations living on such habitat patches make\~up a\~metapopulation. \par
for Examples;\lang1033\par
\lang2057 The source of water\b\~\b0 for lakes in a\~Wisconsin\~lake district is determined by their\b\~\b0 positions in the landscape, which in turn determine their\b\~\b0 hydrologic and chemical responses to drought. \lang1033\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf4\b 2. Write four reasons which relatively have incomplete fossil record. 2\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sa160\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf0 The fossil record is relatively incomplete for several reasons: \par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li2700\sa200\sl360\slmult1\qj\b0\f3\fs22\'b7\tab\f0\fs24 Soft tissues are rarely preserved \par
\f3\fs22\'b7\tab\f0\fs24 Movement of the earth's crust has obliterated and/or covered many fossils \par
\f3\fs22\'b7\tab\f0\fs24 Fossilization takes place only in certain types of habitats and favorable environments \par
\f3\fs22\'b7\tab\f0\fs24 Paleontologists have not dug up every place on earth \par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf4\b 3. Define Hardy-Weinberg principle. 2\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sa160\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf0\b0\lang2057 One of the most fundamental concepts in population genetics, the Hardy-Weinberg principle, states that \ldblquote in a population mating at random in the absence of evolutionary forces, allele frequencies will remain constant.\rdblquote  \lang1033\par
\lang2057 For a population in\~Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium in a situation where there are only two alleles at a particular locus, p + q = 1.0. The frequency of genotypes in a population in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium can be calculated as (p + q)\super 2\nosupersub\~= (p + q) x (p + q) = p\super 2\nosupersub\~+ 2pq + q\super 2\nosupersub\~= 1.0. \lang1033\par
\lang2057 The conditions necessary to maintain\~constent\~allele frequencies in a population are: \lang1033\par

\pard\widctlpar\li2520\sa160\sl360\slmult1\qj\lang2057 random mating,\lang1033\par
\lang2057 no mutations, \lang1033\par
\lang2057 large population size,\lang1033\par
\lang2057 no immigration, and \lang1033\par
\lang2057 equal survival and reproductive rates for all genotypes. \lang1033\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf4\b\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sl360\slmult1\qj 4. How nutrient enrichment says the human is altering the aquatic and terrestrial ecosystem.? 3\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sa160\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf0\ul\lang2057 Nutrient enrichment by humans\par
\ulnone\b0 Vegetative controls appear to be less important in cold\b\~\b0 and/or dry environments. Nutrient loss by stream ecosystems\b\~\b0 is highly pulsed and associated with disturbance by flooding.\lang1033  \lang2057 Nutrient enrichment by humans is altering aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems. Nitrate concentration and export by the earth's major rivers correlate directly with human population density. Human disturbance also increases export of phosphorus from aquatic catchments. Nutrient enrichment appears to be reducing the diversity of plants and fungi in terrestrial ecosystems. Land managers around the world use nutrient loading models to predict and manage the impact of land use on aquatic ecosystems. \lang1033\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf4\b 5. Define and explain optical foraging theory 3\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sa160\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf0\ul\lang2057 Optimal foraging theory\b0\par
\ulnone\b Optimal foraging theory attempts to model how organisms feed as an optimizin process.\~\par
\b0 Evolutionary ecologists predict that if organisms have limited access to energy, natural selection is likely to favor individuals that are more effective at acquiring energy and nutrients. Many animals select food in a way that appears to maximize the rate at which they capture energy. \lang1033\par
\lang2057 Plants appear to allocate energy to roots versus shoots in a way that increases their rate of intake of the resources that limit their growth. Plants in environments with abundant nutrients but little light tend to invest more energy in the growth of stems and leaves and less in roots. In environments rich in light but poor in nutrients, plants tend to invest more energy in the growth of roots. \lang1033\par
\cf4\b 6. Lyell\rquote s Mistake on natural selection. 3\par
\cf0\ul Lyell\rquote s mistake\lang2057\par
\ulnone\b0\lang1033 As a part of his general resistance to direction/ substantial change in geology, Lyell maintained there was no real direction of change in the fossil record either. This was greeted with pretty general astonishment, but Lyell claimed the apparent changes were just due to our having bad (misleading) samples\'85more mammals would soon appear in older formations, etc., as we extend our fossil collections.  Within ten years (1840) it was clear that this was completely untenable.  \tab\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf4\b 7.Species diversity and species richness.\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sa160\sl360\slmult1\qj\tx6210\cf0\ul\lang2057 Species diversity\b0\lang1033\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf1\ulnone\b\f4 Species richness \b0\f5 is the number of different species in a particular community. If we found 30 species in one community, and 300 species in another, the second community would have much higher species richness than the first. Communities with the highest species richness tend to be found in areas near the equator, which have lots of solar energy (supporting high primary productivity), warm temperatures, large amounts of rainfall, and little seasonal change.\par
Communities with the lowest species richness lie near the poles, which get less solar energy and are colder, drier, and less amenable to life\par
\b\f4 Species diversity \b0\f5 is a measure of community complexity. It is a function of both the number of different species in the community (species richness) and their relative abundances (species evenness). Larger numbers of species and more even abundances of species lead to higher species diversity. For example: \f6\bullet\f5  A forest community with 20 different kinds of trees would have greater species diversity than a forest community with only 5 kinds of trees (assuming that the tree species were even in abundance in both cases)\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf4\b\f0 8.Mutualistic relationship.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl360\slmult1\qj\par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sl360\slmult1\qj 9. Community structure define.2\par
\cf0\lang2057 Community structure\b0\~is essentially the composition of a community, including the number of species in that community and their relative numbers^1\u8203?1\u8203?\u8203?start superscript, 1, end superscript. It can also be interpreted more broadly, to include all of the patterns of interaction between these different Species.\cf4\b\lang1033\par
10.Effect of exotic predators on food web. 5\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa160\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf0\ul\lang2057 Exotic predators and the structure of food webs\par
\ulnone Exotic predators can collapse and simplify the structure of food webs\b0 . Introduced fishes have devastated the native fishes of\~Lake\~Atitlan\~and\~Gatun\~Lake\~in\~Central America. \lang1033\par
\ul\b\lang2057 Examples\par
\ulnone\b0 The influence of the Nile perch on the fish community of\~Lake Victoria\~is enmeshed with massive changes in the lake's ecosystem. Introduction of the Nile perch is rapidly reducing the species-rich\lang1033\par
\lang2057  fish fauna of\~Lake Victoria\~to a community dominated by a handful of species. \lang1033\par
\ul\b\lang2057 Humans\par
\ulnone\b0 Humans have acted as keystone species in communities. People have long manipulated food webs both as a consequence of their own feeding activities and by introducing or deleting species from existing food webs. In addition, many of these manipulations have been focused on keystone species. Hunters in tropical rain forests have been responsible for removing keystone animal species from large areas of the rain forests of Central and\~South America. Chinese farmers have used ants as keystone predators to control pests in citrus orchards for over 1,700 years. \par

\pard\widctlpar\li360\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf4\b\lang1033\par
