\widctlpar\fi-360\sl360\slmult1\cf4\b What are the processes of the ecosystem influenced by landscape structure? 2\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl360\slmult1\cf0\highlight0\b0 Landscape structure influences processes such as the flow of energy, materials, and species between the ecosystems within a landscape.\cf1\highlight3\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\sl360\slmult1\cf0\highlight0\f1 2.\tab\cf4\highlight3\b\f0 Define Hardy-Weinberg principle. 2\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf0\highlight0\b0\lang2057 One of the most fundamental concepts in population genetics, the Hardy-Weinberg principle, states that \ldblquote in a population mating at random in the absence of evolutionary forces, allele frequencies will remain constant.\rdblquote  \lang1033\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\sl276\slmult1\f1 3.\tab\cf4\highlight3\b\f0\fs23 How nutrient enrichment says the human is altering the aquatic and terrestrial ecosystem? 3\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl360\slmult1\qj\cf0\highlight0\b0\fs24\lang2057 Nutrient loss by stream ecosystems\b\~\b0 is highly pulsed and associated with disturbance by flooding. Nutrient enrichment by humans is altering aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems. \b\i Nitrate\b0\i0  concentration and export by the earth's major rivers correlate directly with human population density. Human disturbance also increases export of \b\i phosphorus\b0\i0  from aquatic catchments. Nutrient enrichment appears to be reducing the diversity of plants and fungi in terrestrial ecosystems.\lang1033\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\sl276\slmult1\f1 4.\tab\cf4\highlight3\b\f0 Define and explain optical foraging theory 3\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl240\slmult1\qj\cf0\highlight0\lang2057 Optimal foraging theory attempts to model how organisms feed as an optimizing process.\~\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl360\slmult1\qj\b0 Evolutionary ecologists predict that if organisms have limited access to energy, natural selection is likely to favor individuals that are more effective at acquiring energy and nutrients. Many animals select food in a way that appears to maximize the rate at which they capture energy. \lang1033\par
\lang2057 Plants appear to allocate energy to roots versus shoots in a way that increases their rate of intake of the resources that limit their growth. Plants in environments with abundant nutrients but little light tend to invest more energy in the growth of stems and leaves and less in roots. In environments rich in light but poor in nutrients, plants tend to invest more energy in the growth of roots.\cf1\highlight3\b\lang1033\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\sa200\sl276\slmult1\cf0\highlight0\b0\f1 5.\tab\cf4\highlight3\b\f0 Lyell\rquote s Mistake on natural selection. 3\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\cf0\highlight0\b0 As a part of his general resistance to direction/ substantial change in geology, Lyell maintained there was no real direction of change in the fossil record either. This was greeted with pretty general astonishment, but Lyell claimed the apparent changes were just due to our having bad (misleading) samples\'85more mammals would soon appear in older formations, etc., as we extend our fossil collections.  Within ten years (1840) it was clear that this was completely untenable.  \cf1\highlight3\b\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\sl276\slmult1\cf0\highlight0\b0\f1 6.\tab\cf4\highlight3\b\f0 Explain with example how exotic predators disturb the structure of food web. 5\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl240\slmult1\qj\cf0\highlight0\b0 Exotic predators can collapse and simplify the structure of food webs. Introduced fishes have devastated the native fishes of Lake Atitlan and Gatun Lake in Central America.\par
\b\f12 Example:\par
\b0 The influence of the Nile perch on the fish community of Lake Victoria is enmeshed with massive changes in the lake's ecosystem.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl240\slmult1\qj Introduction of the Nile perch is rapidly reducing the species-rich fish fauna of Lake Victoria to a community dominated by a handful of species.\cf1\highlight3\b\f0\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\sa200\sl276\slmult1\cf0\highlight0\b0\f1 7.\tab\cf1\highlight3\b\f0 How evolution of mutualism took place, how humans developed relationship with other\line species. 10\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl240\slmult1\cf5\highlight0\f8 Evolution of Mutualism\par
\b0\f9\bullet\f10  \f8 Theory predicts that mutualism will evolve where the benefits of mutualism exceed the costs. \b\f7 Keeler \b0\f8 built a cost-benefit model for the evolution and persistence of facultative plantant protection mutualisms in which the benefits of the mutualism to the plant are represented in terms of the proportion of the plant's energy budget that ants protect from damage by herbivores. \par
\f9\bullet\f10  \f8 The model assesses the costs of the mutualism to the plant in terms of the proportion of the plant's energy budget invested in extrafloral nectaries and the water, carbohydrates, and amino acids contained in the nectar. The model predicts that the mutualism will be favored where there are high densities of ants and potential herbivores and where the effectiveness of alternative defenses are low.\par
\b Humans in Mutualism\par
\b0\f9\bullet\f10  \f8 Humans have developed a variety of mutualistic relationships with other species, but one of the most spectacular is that between the greater honeyguide and the traditional honey gatherers of Africa. In this apparently ancient mutualism, humans and honeyguides engage in elaborate communication and cooperation with clear benefit to both partners. \par
\f9\bullet\f10  \f8 The mutualism offers the human side a higher rate of discovery of bees' nests, while the honeyguide gains access to nests that it could not raid without human help. Careful observations have documented that the honeyguide informs the honey gatherers of the direction and distance to bees' nests as well as of their arrival at the nest.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\cf1\highlight3\b\f0\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\sl276\slmult1\cf0\highlight0\b0\f1 8.\tab\cf4\highlight3\b\f0 Cuvier\rquote s advantage ?2\par

\pard\widctlpar\sl240\slmult1\cf5\highlight0\b0 Cuvier\rquote s anatomical and classificational work was impeccable.\par
He identified four basic body plans for animals\f2\endash\f0  vertebrates, mollusks, arthropods, and radiate (jellyfish & others).\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl240\slmult1 With these groups clearly distinguished, the chain of being was no longer part of biology.\par

