\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\cf0\highlight0\b0\f13\fs22\lang2057  The Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium model helps identify evolutionary forces that can change gene frequencies in populations. Because evolution involves changes in gene frequencies in a population, a thorough understanding of evolution must include the area of genetics known as population genetics.  \par
\b Hardy-Weinberg principle     2\par
\b0\f14\bullet\f13  One of the most fundamental concepts in population genetics, the Hardy-Weinberg principle, states that  \f14\bullet\f13  in a population mating at random in the absence of evolutionary forces, allele feuecies will eai costat. \par
\f14\bullet\f13  For a population in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium in a situation where there are only two alleles at a particular locus, p + q = 1.0. The frequency of genotypes in a population in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium can be calculated as (p + q)2 = (p + q) x (p + q) = p2 + 2pq + q2 = 1.0.  \par
Hardy-Weinberg principle \par
\f14\bullet\f13  The conditions necessary to maintain constent allele frequencies in a population are:  \f14\endash\f13  (1) random mating, \f14\endash\f13  (2) no mutations,  \f14\endash\f13  (3) large population size, \f14\endash\f13  (4) no immigration, and  \f14\endash\f13  (5) equal survival and reproductive rates for all genotypes.  \f14\bullet\f13  When a population is not in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, the Hardy-Weinberg principle helps us to identify the evolutionary forces that may be in play.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\ul\b\f0 Nutrient enrichment by humans              3\par
\ulnone\b0 Vegetative controls appear to be less important in cold\b\~\b0 and/or dry environments. Nutrient loss by stream ecosystems\b\~\b0 is highly pulsed and associated with disturbance by flooding.Nutrient enrichment by humans is altering aquatic and terrestrial ecosystems. Nitrate concentration and export by the earth's major rivers correlate directly with human population density. Human disturbance also increases export of phosphorus from aquatic catchments. Nutrient enrichment appears to be reducing the diversity of plants and fungi in terrestrial ecosystems. Land managers around the world use nutrient loading models to predict and manage the impact of land use on aquatic ecosystems. \par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f13 Q    \par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\ul\b\f0 Landscape structure influences Ecological processes       2\b0\par
\ulnone\b Landscape structure influences processes such as the flow of energy, materials, and species between the ecosystems within a landscape\b0 .\~Landscape ecologists have proposed that landscape structure, especially the size, number,\~and isolation of habitat patches, can influence the movement\~of organisms between potentially suitable habitats. The\~group of subpopulations living on such habitat patches make\~up a\~metapopulation. \par
\ul\b Examples\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\ulnone\b0\f3\lang1033\'b7\tab\f0\lang2057 Studies of the movements of small mammals\b\~\b0 in a prairie landscape show that a smaller proportion of individuals\~moves\~in more fragmented landscape but that the\b\~\b0 individuals that do move will move farther.The local population density of the Glanville fritillary butterfly,\~\b\i Melitaea\~cinxia\b0\i0 , is\~lower\~on larger and on isolated habitat patches.\b\~\b0 Small populations of this butterfly and desert bighorn sheep\b\~\b0 are more vulnerable to local extinction. \lang1033\par
\f3\'b7\tab\f0\lang2057 The source of water\b\~\b0 for lakes in a\~Wisconsin\~lake district is determined by their\b\~\b0 positions in the landscape, which in turn determine their\b\~\b0 hydrologic and chemical responses to drought. \lang1033\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f13\lang2057 Q\par

