\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\ul\b\f0 Optimal foraging theory        3\b0\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone\b Optimal foraging theory attempts to model how organisms feed as an optimizin process.\~\par
\b0 Evolutionary ecologists predict that if organisms have limited access to energy, natural selection is likely to favor individuals that are more effective at acquiring energy and nutrients. Many animals select food in a way that appears to maximize the rate at which they capture energy. \par
Plants appear to allocate energy to roots versus shoots in a way that increases their rate of intake of the resources that limit their growth. Plants in environments with abundant nutrients but little light tend to invest more energy in the growth of stems and leaves and less in roots. In environments rich in light but poor in nutrients, plants tend to invest more energy in the growth of roots.\par
Q\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\ul\b Proterozoic Animal Fossils      3\par

\pard\widctlpar\fi-360\li720\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone\b0\f3\lang1033\'b7\tab\f0 Although scarce, a few animal fossils older than those of the Ediacaran fauna are known.\par
\f3\'b7\tab\f0 A jellyfish-like impression is present in rocks 2000 m below the Ediacara Hills Pound Quartzite. \par
\f3\'b7\tab\f0 Burrows in many areas presumably made by worms, occur in rocks at least 700 million years old. \par
\f3\'b7\tab\f0 Wormlike and algae fossils come from 700 to 900 million-year-old rocks in China but the identity and age of these \ldblquote fossils\rdblquote  has been questioned\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\ul\b\lang2057 Soft Bodies\par
\ulnone\b0 All known Proterozoic animals were soft-bodied, but there is some evidence that the earliest stages in the origin of skeletons was underway. Even some Ediacaran animals may have had a chitinous carapace and others appear to have had areas of calcium carbonate. The odd creature known as Kimberella from the latest Proterozoic of Russia had a tough outer covering similar to that of some present-day marine invertebrates\ul\b .\par
Latest Proterozoic Kimberella\par
\ulnone\b0 Kimberella, an animal from latest Proterozoic rocks in Russia. Exactly what Kimberella was remains uncertain. Some think it was a sluglike creature whereas others think it was more like a mollusk\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ul\b Durable Skeletons\par
\ulnone\b0 Latest Proterozoic fossils of minute scraps of shell-like material and small tooth like denticles and spicules, presumably from sponges indicate that several animals with skeletons or at least partial skeletons existed. More durable skeletons of silica, calcium carbonate, and chitin (a complex organic substance) did not appear in abundance until the beginning of the Phanerozoic Eon 545 million years ago --- Cambrian age\par
Q\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\tx1920\ul\b Lyell\rquote s mistake\tab 5\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone\b0 As a part of his general resistance to direction/ substantial change in geology, Lyell  maintained there was no real direction of change in the fossil record either. This was greeted with pretty general astonishment, but Lyell claimed the apparent changes were just due to our having bad (misleading) samples\'85more mammals would soon appear in older formations, etc., as we extend our fossil collections.  Within ten years (1840) it was clear that this was completely untenable\par
Q\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\ul\b Exotic predators and the structure of food webs              5\par
\ulnone Exotic predators can collapse and simplify the structure of food webs\b0 . Introduced fishes have devastated the native fishes of\~Lake\~Atitlan\~and\~Gatun\~Lake\~in\~Central America. \par
\ul\b Examples\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ulnone\b0 The influence of the Nile perch on the fish community of\~Lake Victoria\~is enmeshed with massive changes in the lake's ecosystem. Introduction of the Nile perch is rapidly reducing the species-rich\par
 fish fauna of\~Lake Victoria\~to a community dominated by a handful of species. \par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\ul\b Humans\par
\ulnone\b0 Humans have acted as keystone species in communities. People have long manipulated food webs both as a consequence of their own feeding activities and by introducing or deleting species from existing food webs. In addition, many of these manipulations have been focused on keystone species. Hunters in tropical rain forests have been responsible for removing keystone animal species from large areas of the rain forests of Central and\~South America. Chinese farmers have used ants as keystone predators to control pests in citrus orchards for over 1,700 years. \par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f13 Q\par
\ul\b\f0 Endosymbiosis and the Origin of Eukaryotic Cells              5\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\ulnone\b0 Formed from several prokaryotic cells . In a symbiotic relationship Symbiosis involving a prolonged association of two or more dissimilar organisms, is quite common today. In many cases both symbionts benefit from the association as occurs in lichens, once thought to be plants but actually symbiotic fungi and algae\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ul\b Evidence for Endosymbiosis\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\ulnone\b0 Supporting evidence for endosymbiosis comes from studies of living eukaryotic cells containing internal structures called organelles, such as mitochondria and plastics which contain their own genetic material. In addition, prokaryotic cells synthesize proteins as a single system, whereas eukaryotic cells are a combination of protein-synthesizing systems.\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ul\b Organelles Capable of Protein Synthesis\par

\pard\widctlpar\sa200\sl276\slmult1\qj\ulnone\b0 That is, some of the organelles within eukaryotic cells are capable of protein synthesis. These organelles with their own genetic material and protein-synthesizing capabilities are thought to have been free-living bacteria that entered into a symbiotic relationship, eventually giving rise to eukaryotic cells\par

